---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../lib/sites';
import { articleJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd, faqPageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.main}/hookah-charcoal-manufacturing/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How can I tell whether a supplier actually manufactures or just repacks?',
    a: 'Ask three process questions: where the carbonised shell comes from, what pressing equipment is used, and how drying is controlled. Then ask for a QC sheet whose batch number matches the packing list. A factory can produce that record; a trader usually forwards a datasheet instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What density should hookah charcoal have?',
    a: 'Public process references place pressed shisha briquettes in a working range of roughly 1.2–1.3 t/m³, but the figure is only meaningful with its method. Bulk density decides container loading; particle density describes a single cube. Ask which one is quoted, then confirm by weighing a sample cube and measuring it with calipers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does cube size vary inside one carton?',
    a: 'Nominal size is a die dimension, not a guarantee. Die wear, paste moisture, press pressure and drying shrinkage all move the finished dimension across a run, so a supplier who checks only at the start ships cubes at the edge of the band. Specify a tolerance and a sampling plan, not just a size.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a darker cube a better cube?',
    a: 'No. Colour responds to carbonisation temperature, binder share and drying, and it is not a specification. Two cubes of the same colour can differ in ash, density and burn behaviour. Judge weight, hardness, dimension, ash and a burn test — not shade.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What causes sparks or a chemical smell when lighting?',
    a: 'Sparks usually point to feedstock contamination — sand, shell fibre or foreign material that survived screening. A chemical or solvent note is more often an accelerant coating, an industrial binder, or incomplete carbonisation leaving volatiles. Both are process signals, and both are reasons to test a sample rather than accept a datasheet.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.main}/` },
    { name: 'Hookah Charcoal Manufacturing', url: canonical },
  ]),
  articleJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    headline: 'Hookah Charcoal Manufacturing: Quality, Density and Consistency',
    description:
      'How pressed hookah charcoal is manufactured and controlled — carbonisation, binder, pressing, density, drying and the QC records that hold a batch together.',
    datePublished: '2026-11-14',
    dateModified: '2026-11-14',
  }),
  faqPageJsonLd({ id: `${canonical}#faq`, faqs }),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="Hookah Charcoal Manufacturing: Quality and Consistency"
  description="How pressed hookah charcoal is made and controlled: carbonisation, binder, pressing, density, drying and the QC records that hold a batch together."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Hookah Charcoal Manufacturing', url: '/hookah-charcoal-manufacturing/' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="page-title-split container">
    <div>
      <h1>Hookah Charcoal Manufacturing: Quality, Density and Consistency</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A hookah cube is decided at the press and the dryer, not at the packing table. This guide
        walks the production line stage by stage, shows which parameter each stage fixes, and turns
        the vague claim of “factory quality” into checks a buyer can actually run.
      </p>
      <p class="quote-note">
        Process ranges below are industry references, not one factory’s settings. No independent
        audit report for a named plant is on file.
        <a href="/request-quote/">Request a quote →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="page-title-media">
      <img src="/images/page-manufacturing.webp" alt="Charcoal briquette production line with a press and conveyor — conceptual illustration" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="callout-title">How to read the evidence labels</span>
        <p>
          <span class="badge badge-warn">Supplier-declared</span> means a figure comes from a
          supplier listing or datasheet and has not been independently tested by Charcoal Hub.
          <span class="badge badge-muted">Industry reference</span> means a class-level range from
          public process sources — orientation, not a commitment from any plant.
          <span class="badge badge-accent">Data pending</span> means no figure is published for that
          field. Method definitions live on the
          <a href={`${SITES.testing}/tests/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">testing sub-site</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What Manufacturing Quality Means in Hookah Charcoal</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Buyers often describe quality as a feeling: the cube lights evenly, holds its shape, does
          not smell, and leaves a fine grey ash instead of a crust. Those are real outcomes, but
          they are outputs of a process. A manufacturing claim is only useful when it can be traced
          back to a stage that was controlled and a record that proves it.
        </p>
        <p>
          In practice, “quality” in this product means two different things, and they are frequently
          confused. The first is <strong>conformance</strong>: does this batch meet the declared ash,
          moisture, size and density limits? The second is <strong>consistency</strong>: does the next
          batch meet them too? A single perfect laboratory report answers the first question for one
          lot. Only a run of batch records answers the second.
        </p>
        <p>
          That distinction matters commercially. A buyer who qualifies on one sample and then orders
          a container is trusting a process they have never seen. The sections below are arranged in
          production order so that each claim can be attached to the stage that creates it — and so
          that a plant visit or a video call has a structure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The Line, Stage by Stage</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The conventional line for pressed coconut shell hookah charcoal runs through seven stages.
          Each one leaves a fingerprint on the finished cube, and each one has a characteristic
          failure mode that shows up later as a complaint.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it fixes</th><th>Characteristic failure</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Shell intake and screening</td><td>Ash precursor, contamination</td><td>Sand, fibre or mixed feedstock raises ash and causes sparks</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Carbonisation</td><td>Volatile matter, fixed carbon, yield</td><td>Under-carbonised char smokes and smells; over-carbonised char wastes yield</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Milling and screening</td><td>Particle size distribution, packing behaviour</td><td>A coarse or drifting grind gives soft cubes and uneven burn</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Binder mixing</td><td>Green strength, ash, odour</td><td>Too much binder softens the cube and lifts ash; the wrong binder adds a note</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pressing and cutting</td><td>Density, nominal size, shape</td><td>Die wear drifts dimensions; low pressure gives a crumbly cube</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drying and conditioning</td><td>Moisture, crack formation, hardness</td><td>Fast drying cracks cubes; under-drying ships soft, smoky product</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sorting and packing</td><td>Fines removal, count, barrier</td><td>Fines left in the box read as breakage at arrival</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="meta-line">
        Stage sequence is the general process described in public industry sources; equipment and
        set-points differ between plants. No named factory is described here.
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The order is not arbitrary: a defect introduced at carbonisation cannot be fixed at
          packing, and an under-pressed cube cannot be rescued by longer drying.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Carbonisation: Where Ash and Fixed Carbon Are Decided</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Coconut shells are heated in a kiln, retort or drum with limited oxygen. Public process
          references put the effective window at roughly 600–900&nbsp;°C
          <span class="badge badge-muted">Industry reference</span>. Inside that window, volatile
          matter is driven off as gas and what remains is a carbon skeleton — the material that
          carries fixed carbon.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things are decided here that buyers care about most. The first is
          <strong>volatile matter</strong>. Incomplete carbonisation leaves volatiles behind, and
          those volatiles are the closest laboratory proxy for start-up smoke and odour. A cube made
          from under-carbonised char can look perfect and still taste wrong. The second is
          <strong>ash</strong>, but indirectly: ash is mineral matter from the shell, and because
          carbonisation removes mass, the same minerals become a larger percentage of the finished
          char. Clean, screened shells are therefore the cheapest ash control available.
        </p>
        <p>
          Yield is the commercial counterweight. Public references place the shell-to-charcoal ratio
          at roughly 2–2.5 tonnes of shells per tonne of char
          <span class="badge badge-muted">Industry reference</span>. Push carbonisation harder and
          yield falls; that cost reappears in the price or the binder share.
        </p>
        <p>
          Feedstock moisture matters before the kiln, not after. Process references describe shells
          being dried to roughly 20% or below before carbonisation
          <span class="badge badge-muted">Industry reference</span>, because wet shells carbonise
          slowly and unevenly. A plant that stores shells in the open will produce char that varies
          with the weather — and no downstream stage fully corrects for that.
        </p>
        <p>
          For buyers, the practical question is who owns this stage. Some suppliers carbonise
          in-house; others buy carbonised shell powder and run only the forming line. Neither is
          dishonest, but the second model means ash and volatile matter are inherited from a
          third party, and batch traceability has an extra link. Ask directly, and ask for the char
          supplier’s specification if the answer is “we buy powder”. The
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/how-coconut-shell-charcoal-is-made/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">process explainer</a>
          covers the same chain from the feedstock side.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Milling, Binder and Moisture in the Mix</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Carbonised shell is ground before it can be pressed. The grind is a real specification
          variable even though it rarely appears on a datasheet. A finer, more uniform powder packs
          more tightly and produces a harder green cube, but it also needs more water and can change
          how the finished cube burns. A coarse or drifting grind gives a soft cube, a rough surface
          and inconsistent density inside the piece.
        </p>
        <p>
          The binder is usually food-grade tapioca or cassava starch: the glue that holds the cube
          together and burns away with the fuel. Its share affects green strength, ash after
          combustion and sometimes odour during lighting. More binder makes a tougher cube and a
          higher ash residue, which is why “low ash” and “extra hard” claims belong together.
        </p>
        <p>
          Water in the mix is process water, not product moisture. It makes the paste workable and is
          removed in the dryer. Confusing the two is a common source of bad specifications: a buyer
          asks for “5% moisture” and the supplier measures the wet mix instead of the finished cube.
          Always state that the moisture limit applies to the dried, packed product.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a supplier claims a binderless cube, ask how it holds together without one and what the
          drop-test result is. Some products are pressed from char with enough natural tars to bind;
          many “binderless” claims are simply a different starch that is not declared. Neither answer
          is automatically disqualifying, but the answer should be specific.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Pressing: Density, Die Wear and Size Drift</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The press turns paste into a cube. Pressure is the main lever on density, and density is
          the property a buyer feels first: a dense cube has weight in the hand, survives handling
          and burns longer for its size. Public process references place pressed shisha briquettes in
          a working range around 1.2–1.3&nbsp;t/m³
          <span class="badge badge-muted">Industry reference</span>. Treat that as an orientation
          band, not a purchase limit — it is a class-level figure, not a factory guarantee.
        </p>
        <p>
          Size is set by the die, and dies wear. Across a long production run the cavity enlarges
          slightly, so cubes pressed at the end of a run can measure larger than cubes pressed at the
          start. This is the mechanical reason a nominal “25&nbsp;mm” label is not a tolerance. It is
          also why an in-process dimension check at the start, middle and end of a run tells you more
          than a single measurement on a single sample.
        </p>
        <p>
          Green strength — the strength of the cube before drying — is an intermediate the buyer
          never sees, but it predicts the arrival condition. A cube that is fragile when wet tends to
          crack in the dryer and shed fines in the carton. Press pressure, binder share and particle
          size all feed into it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Drying and Conditioning: The Arrival Risk</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Drying is the least glamorous stage and the one most likely to decide whether the container
          arrives in sellable condition. Public process references describe finished hookah cubes
          drying to roughly 6–8% moisture
          <span class="badge badge-muted">Industry reference</span>, with some buyers specifying a
          tighter ceiling at the factory gate. The exact target matters less than the control: a
          uniform, measured endpoint beats a nominal number achieved unevenly.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two failure modes sit on either side of the target. <strong>Under-drying</strong> leaves
          water in the cube: it lights slowly, smokes more, weighs more than it should and can
          develop mould in a warm container. <strong>Over-drying</strong> — especially fast, high-heat
          drying — creates a moisture gradient, and the shrinkage stress shows up as hairline cracks
          after handling.
        </p>
        <p>
          Drying method changes uniformity. Oven or tunnel drying gives a controlled, repeatable
          curve; sun drying depends on weather and turns out a batch that varies day to day. For a
          container order, the difference is not cosmetic: it is the difference between a moisture
          figure you can put in a contract and one you hope holds.
        </p>
        <p>
          Charcoal is hygroscopic, so a cube that leaves at 6% moves toward equilibrium with the
          humidity around it in transit. Packaging therefore belongs in the moisture specification,
          and an arrival check belongs in the purchase terms. The
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-moisture-explained/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">moisture guide</a>
          sets out the measurement and storage detail.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Density: Three Different Numbers</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          “Density” is used for at least three different measurements in this trade, and a datasheet
          rarely says which one it means. The numbers are not interchangeable, and comparing them
          across suppliers is a reliable way to reach a wrong conclusion.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Measure</th><th>What it describes</th><th>What it decides</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Bulk density</td>
              <td>Mass of a loosely filled or settled volume of cubes, typically kg/m³</td>
              <td>Container loading and freight per tonne</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Particle (apparent) density</td>
              <td>Mass of one piece divided by its envelope volume</td>
              <td>How much fuel sits in a cube of a given size</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Piece mass</td>
              <td>Weight of a single cube, in grams</td>
              <td>The quickest field check, and the basis for pieces per kilogram</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="meta-line">
        Bulk density is measured under ISO 17828 procedures or a container-fill method; particle
        density uses a different method entirely. See the
        <a href={`${SITES.testing}/tests/density/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">density method page</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The consequences run in different directions. Higher bulk density fits more tonnes into a
          container, which lowers freight per tonne — a logistics win that says nothing about how the
          cube burns. Higher particle density means more carbon per piece, and generally a longer,
          steadier burn. A heavier piece can be either, or simply absorbed water.
        </p>
        <p>
          That last point catches buyers repeatedly. Water adds weight without adding fuel. A cube
          that has taken up moisture feels dense and performs badly — slow to light, smoky, and short
          on usable heat. Density figures should therefore always be read next to moisture, and a
          measured density should state whether it is on a dry basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a practical check, weigh ten random cubes and measure them with calipers. The spread in
          piece mass at a fixed nominal size is a direct read on press consistency, and it takes two
          minutes. The
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-density-and-heat-management/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">density and heat management guide</a>
          connects the number to session behaviour.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Consistency: Batch Variation and What It Looks Like</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Consistency is a process property, and it fails in two directions. <strong>Within a
          batch</strong>, the symptoms are visible: a spread of cube sizes in one carton, a mix of
          shades, some pieces noticeably heavier, cracks or chipped corners. <strong>Between
          batches</strong>, the symptoms are quieter and more expensive: ash drifts up half a point,
          burn time shortens, a customer notices the session changed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The sources of drift are ordinary: a new shell supplier changes the mineral load, a
          different char lot changes volatile matter, a binder delivery changes green strength, humid
          weather changes drying, a worn die changes size. None is a scandal; all are invisible
          unless the plant records them.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is why a single certificate is weak evidence and three consecutive batch records are
          strong. Ask for the same fields across three batches — ash, moisture, volatile matter,
          fixed carbon, size check, density or piece mass — and look at the spread rather than the
          average. A supplier whose three batches sit in a narrow band is controlling the line. A
          supplier whose values wander is running a process that happens to have produced a good
          sample.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two visual shortcuts are worth discarding. Colour is not a grade: it responds to
          carbonisation temperature, binder share and drying, and two cubes of the same shade can
          differ in ash and density. Surface gloss is not a guarantee either — a polished look can
          come from a die, a coating or a tumbling step, and none of those is a specification. Judge
          mass, dimension, hardness, ash and a burn test; leave appearance out of the qualification.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The QC Plan: Stage Gates and Records</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A workable plan is short: a check at each stage where a parameter is fixed, recorded against
          a batch number so it can be traced to the cartons it covers. The table is a buyer’s version
          of that plan — what to require, not a claim about any supplier’s practice.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Gate</th><th>Check</th><th>Record that makes it useful</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Shell intake</td><td>Source, cleanliness, moisture before carbonisation</td><td>Supplier lot, date, moisture reading</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Carbonised char</td><td>Volatile matter, ash, appearance, unburnt shell</td><td>Kiln run ID and result per run</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Milled powder</td><td>Particle size, uniformity, foreign matter</td><td>Sieve result or grind setting log</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mix</td><td>Binder type and share, water addition</td><td>Recipe version and batch weight</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pressed green</td><td>Piece mass, dimensions, green strength</td><td>Start / middle / end-of-run measurements</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dried</td><td>Moisture, cracks, hardness</td><td>Dryer curve and final moisture per lot</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Packed</td><td>Fines, count per box, seal integrity</td><td>Carton count and sample photos</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pre-shipment</td><td>Fresh sample, dimensions, moisture, DG labels</td><td>Inspection report with container number</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="meta-line">
        Gate list is a recommended buyer framework. Method references: ASTM D1762 / ASTM D3172–D3175
        for proximate analysis, GB/T 12496 for Chinese charcoal reports, ISO 17831-1 and ASTM D440
        for durability and drop behaviour.
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The record is the part that gets skipped. A QC sheet with numbers but no batch ID cannot be
          tied to the cartons in front of you, and a test result from “last month” describes a
          different lot. Ask for the batch number on the packing list to match the QC sheet, and
          reject the shipment if it does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sampling discipline matters as much as the test. Samples taken from the top layer of a
          pallet are not a sample of the load. A working plan takes pieces from several cartons
          across the pallet, and for a container, from more than one position in the stow. The
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/pre-shipment-inspection-charcoal/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">pre-shipment inspection guide</a>
          sets out the sequence, including the dangerous-goods labelling check.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Process Claims, Defects and What They Reveal</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A defect is more useful than a compliment. Each symptom below points to a stage, and the
          stage tells you which question to ask. The mapping narrows the conversation; it is not a
          diagnosis.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely stage</th><th>Question to ask</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Cubes crumble or shed dust in the box</td><td>Pressing, binder, drying</td><td>What is the drop-test or durability result for this size, and what fines limit applies?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hairline cracks after arrival</td><td>Drying</td><td>What is the dryer curve, and is moisture measured before or after conditioning?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Size spread inside one carton</td><td>Pressing, die wear</td><td>Are dimensions checked at the end of the run, and what tolerance band is guaranteed?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sparks during lighting</td><td>Shell intake, screening</td><td>How is feedstock screened for sand and foreign matter?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Chemical or solvent note</td><td>Binder, coating, carbonisation</td><td>What exactly is in the binder, and is there any accelerant layer?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Smoky start, ash-heavy session</td><td>Carbonisation, moisture</td><td>Volatile matter and moisture for this batch, with method and basis?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Uneven glow across a cube</td><td>Mixing, pressing</td><td>Is density checked within a piece, or only as a batch average?</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The same logic applies to marketing language. “Smokeless” is not a measurable property —
          volatile matter and a session test are. “100% natural” says nothing about the binder unless
          the binder is named. “Export grade” has no legal definition; “premium” is a price signal.
          None of these is proof of a problem; they are simply not evidence. Evidence is a batch
          record, a named method, a stated basis and a sample you burned yourself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Specifying Quality in the Purchase Order</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The manufacturing detail only becomes enforceable when it is written into the order. A
          workable annex has five parts: the field, the limit, the method, the basis and the status
          (maximum, minimum or typical). Anything missing is a conversation, not a term.
        </p>
        <p>
          Start with the fields that decide the product: cube size and tolerance, piece mass or
          density, moisture at packing, ash maximum, volatile matter maximum, fixed carbon minimum,
          and a named binder. Add the sensory items you can evaluate — a blind session test for odour
          and ignition — and record the result rather than describing it in adjectives.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then define the batch. One production run per shipment, or a declared split, is far easier
          to inspect than a blended load. Agree the sampling plan, the laboratory or method, the
          acceptance limits and the remedy: replacement, credit or rework. A specification without a
          remedy is a wish.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a private-label order, make the pre-production sample a formal gate. A screen proof will
          not show registration on moisture-resistant board; a first article from the actual line
          will. The <a href="/private-label/">private label process</a> covers artwork, MOQ and
          approval; the
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/private-label-charcoal/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">private-label guide</a>
          covers the commercial side.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, inspect before shipment. Sample from the middle of the load, verify carton
          integrity and UN 1361 Class 4.2 marking, and photograph the lot with the container number
          visible. A photo set is the cheapest dispute insurance in the trade, and it costs nothing
          to require.
        </p>
        <p>
          We publish no price list or fixed MOQ, because both depend on specification, packaging and
          destination. Describe the cube, the volume and the market. Any figure without evidence — a
          density reading, a batch history, a binder declaration — stays
          <span class="badge badge-accent">Data pending</span>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          If you are still selecting a product, start with the
          <a href="/products/hookah-charcoal/">hookah charcoal page</a> or the
          <a href="/products/coconut-shell-charcoal/">coconut shell category</a>. If you have a
          specification, the next useful step is a sample and a quote. If you are auditing a plant,
          take the gate table above as the interview script.
        </p>
        <p>
          The declared values behind this product family live in the
          <a href={`${SITES.data}/products/25mm-natural-coconut-shell-hookah-charcoal/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">25 mm product record</a>,
          with the same status legend applied to every field. The
          <a href={`${SITES.testing}/tests/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">testing sub-site</a>
